{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's quantitative law rests on an unsupported and generally invalid inference: from 'expected number of such obstruction structures is exp(Θ(n))' it concludes 'their basins cover all but an exp(-Θ(n)) fraction of initial colorings.' Expected number of stable states does not bound total basin measure; exponentially many stable states can each have exponentially small basins, leaving consensus probability bounded away from 0 or even tending to 1. Moreover, the asynchronous update order is stochastic, so a single initial coloring can reach different absorbing states depending on update order, making the deterministic 'basin' partition ill-defined. The target gives no argument for the basin coverage claim, so the exp(-Θ(n)) consensus law is unsupported.","target":"8716c67d078900e52f78eff3bceed0ad07e6955f35d29adbb6e7a3ad5e339ed6"}]}